IMPORTANT ANNOUNCEMENT REGARDING ASSIGNMENT 4:

Dear students,
I hope this message finds you well. I wanted to address some common questions that I've gotten so far related to the assignment:

Question 1 (pacman):
Some of you have inquired about obtaining single-character input without repeatedly pressing the enter key.

To achive this on UBUNTU, you can utilize the following code snippet:
// LIBRARY REQUIRED #include <unistd.h>
// set system in raw mode
system("stty raw");
//raw mode logic here
move = getchar();
// reset system setting
system("stty cooked");

To achieve this on WINDOWS, you can utilize the following code snippet:
// LIBRARY REQUIRED #include <windows.h>
// set system in raw mode
HANDLE hStdin = GetStdHandle(STD_INPUT_HANDLE);
DWORD mode;
GetConsoleMode(hStdin, &mode);
SetConsoleMode(hStdin, mode & (~ENABLE_ECHO_INPUT) & (~ENABLE_LINE_INPUT));
// Your raw mode logic here
move = getchar();
// To restore to normal mode
SetConsoleMode(hStdin, mode);

Question 2 (Custom printing pattern):
For those of you who may have observed that the provided example features a matrix with 5 rows and 4 columns, while the accompanying note suggests that your code should accommodate any SQUARE matrix of size n x n, kindly DISREGARD this note. Your code is expected to function effectively for all possible combinations of matrix sizes.

Question 3 (3D array quadrant manipulation):
There has been a clarification regarding the calculation of the random numbers in your matrices. Please disregard the previously mentioned formula for determining the random numbers in all instances. The previous formula stated that the random numbers be calculated using the formula (elements in the quadrant divided by 8), DISREGARD this. For your ease, just calculate the number of random numbers using the following formula, ((n x n) / 8). For instance, if your matrix size is 3, 8, 8, the calculation would be ((8 x 8) / 8) = 8. Similarly, for a matrix size of 3, 16, 16, apply the formula as ((16 x 16) / 8) = 32.
Now for the part where you have to move the values forward to the 2nd and 1st dimensions, use the formula ((n x n) / 2). For instance, if your matrix size is 3, 8, 8, the calculation would be ((8 x 8) / 2) = 32. Similarly, for a matrix size of 3, 16, 16, apply the formula as ((16 x 16) / 2) = 128.
This is simply changed for your ease! FOR THOSE WHO HAVE ALREADY DONE IT USING THE PREVIOUS METHOD, THAT'S FINE AS WELL.

Question 4 (Profit maximization):
For those seeking clarification on the optimal window size in this particular question, it is imperative to note that the size of the window is DEPENDENT upon the VALUES of your generated matrix. The window size will vary, ranging from 2 to length of array i.e. it could be of length 2, 7 or even 20, depending entirely on the values of your array. This is what the task is all about; to identify that specific window in which your profit is MAXIMUM.

Should you have any inquiries or require additional guidance, please do not hesitate to reach out. Thank you!
